; DB25 physical planner spec - the versioned IDL (design D1/D7).
;
; The single source of truth for the physical operator catalog and the reference
; execution-capability profile. The planner interprets this spec rather than
; embedding operator knowledge in code; the conformance check (src/spec.cpp)
; asserts the spec and the code agree - every operator the planner can emit is
; declared here, with the arity the IR expects, and marked executable by the
; reference profile. Adding an operator starts by editing this file.
;
; Format: s-expressions (zero-dependency, diffable, the same discipline the rest
; of DB25 already uses). `;` begins a line comment.

(physical-spec
  (version 0)

  ; The physical operator catalog. `arity` is the number of child inputs; `kind`
  ; is a coarse pipeline classification.
  ;
  ; `kind` IS AND REMAINS A SUMMARY, and increment 4.1 is where that stopped
  ; being a placeholder and became a statement. Where rows stop is a property of
  ; an EDGE, not of an operator: a hash join MATERIALIZES its build input and
  ; STREAMS its probe input, and which of the two is the build side is chosen per
  ; plan by the search, so no label on this line can be right for both. The
  ; per-edge answer lives in pipeline.hpp's `edge_kind`, where it can read the
  ; candidate; a test declares it for every input of every operator, and another
  ; asserts the two do not contradict each other for the operators where a single
  ; label CAN be right.
  ;
  ; So each operator also declares `edges`: one word per INPUT, saying what
  ; happens to rows arriving on it.
  ;
  ;   streaming    - they flow into this operator's output within one loop
  ;   materialized - this whole input is buffered before anything is emitted
  ;   rescanned    - this input is RE-EXECUTED, once per row of another input
  ;   separate     - its own loop, feeding the same consumer as a sibling
  ;
  ; `kind` is then DERIVED from `edges`: an operator is a pipeline-breaker
  ; exactly when it materializes an input, which is the question an executor asks
  ; when it wants to know where the memory goes. Conformance checks both - that
  ; `edge_kind()` in the code agrees with `edges` here, and that `kind` is the
  ; summary `edges` implies - so the three cannot drift apart.
  ;
  ; Nine labels were wrong when this was first checked: a merge join, the three
  ; nested loops and the recursive fixpoint were called breakers though they
  ; buffer nothing, and the four write operators were called breakers though rows
  ; pass straight through them into a table. They END pipelines, which is a real
  ; thing and is what `edges` now says; they do not BREAK them in the sense that
  ; costs memory.
  (operators
    (operator (name SeqScan)       (arity 0) (kind access-path))
    (operator (name Filter)        (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    (operator (name Project)       (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    (operator (name HashJoin)      (arity 2) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges streaming materialized))
    ; MergeJoin requires both inputs sorted on the join keys and preserves that
    ; order on its output - so it is cheaper per row than HashJoin when the order
    ; is already there, and dearer once a Sort must be enforced to get it.
    (operator (name MergeJoin)     (arity 2) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming separate))
    ; NestedLoopJoin needs no equi-key, which makes it the ONLY legal
    ; implementation of a cross product or a purely non-equi join - and its cost
    ; is quadratic in the inputs, so it never wins where a keyed join applies.
    (operator (name NestedLoopJoin) (arity 2) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming rescanned))
    ; Limit passes at most `limit` rows through after discarding `offset` of
    ; them. Order-SENSITIVE: which rows survive depends entirely on the order its
    ; input arrives in, which is why the planner may not push an order
    ; requirement through it.
    (operator (name Limit)         (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    ; Two implementations of GROUP BY. HashAggregate works on any input and
    ; produces no order; StreamingAggregate needs its input already sorted on the
    ; grouping keys, is cheaper per row, and keeps that order on its output.
    (operator (name HashAggregate)      (arity 1) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges materialized))
    (operator (name StreamingAggregate) (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    ; Window consumes its input sorted by (PARTITION BY ++ ORDER BY) and appends
    ; one column per window function, emitting in that same order.
    (operator (name Window)             (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    ; GROUPING SETS / ROLLUP / CUBE in one pass, hashing each row into every set's
    ; table. No streaming counterpart: different sets want different orderings.
    (operator (name HashGroupingSets)   (arity 1) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges materialized))
    ; Two implementations of SELECT DISTINCT, the same trade as the aggregates.
    (operator (name HashDistinct)       (arity 1) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges materialized))
    (operator (name StreamingDistinct)  (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    ; Set operations. UnionAll compares nothing; HashSetOp builds from the right
    ; and probes with the left. They are NOT alternatives for one another -
    ; applicability, not cost, selects between them.
    (operator (name UnionAll)           (arity 2) (kind pipeline) (edges separate separate))
    (operator (name HashSetOp)          (arity 2) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges streaming materialized))
    ; A literal row set, and the synthetic single row of a FROM-less SELECT.
    (operator (name ValuesScan)         (arity 0) (kind access-path))
    ; Semi and anti joins (EXISTS / IN, NOT EXISTS). Both emit the LEFT schema
    ; only and emit each qualifying left row ONCE however many right rows match,
    ; which is why they are their own operators rather than a join plus a distinct.
    ; Four rather than two with a mode flag: they are exact complements, and an
    ; operator that does not say which it is reads the same as the other.
    (operator (name HashSemiJoin)       (arity 2) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges streaming materialized))
    (operator (name HashAntiJoin)       (arity 2) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges streaming materialized))
    (operator (name NestedLoopSemiJoin) (arity 2) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming rescanned))
    (operator (name NestedLoopAntiJoin) (arity 2) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming rescanned))
    ; Sort is BOTH the implementation of a logical Sort (ORDER BY) and the
    ; enforcer inserted to establish a required order - one operator, because they
    ; are one operation; only who asked for it differs.
    (operator (name Sort)          (arity 1) (kind pipeline-breaker) (edges materialized))
    ; A pure enforcer: inserted to establish a required storage format.
    (operator (name FormatConvert) (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    ; The third enforcer: moves rows between nodes to establish a required
    ; DISTRIBUTION - repartition by hash, broadcast, or gather to one place. Its
    ; edge is `separate` rather than `materialized`: the sending and receiving
    ; sides are two loops, but nothing is buffered between them.
    (operator (name Exchange)      (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges separate))
    ; WITH RECURSIVE. RecursiveFixpoint takes the anchor and the recursive term as
    ; its two children - the anchor is evaluated once, the term once per iteration,
    ; which is why they are separate children rather than one hidden input.
    ; WorkingTableScan is the recursive self-reference: a leaf whose rows come from
    ; the previous iteration, and the only operator in this catalog fed from
    ; elsewhere in its own plan.
    (operator (name RecursiveFixpoint)  (arity 2) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming rescanned))
    (operator (name WorkingTableScan)   (arity 0) (kind access-path))
    ; CREATE TABLE AS: materialize the input stream into a new table.
    (operator (name CreateTableAs)      (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    ; The write path. One input each - the source query for an Insert, the
    ; scanned and filtered target rows for an Update or a Delete - and each emits
    ; the AFFECTED rows, which a RETURNING clause then projects. The target
    ; relation is a NAME on the operator, not a child: it is written, not read.
    ; Three operators rather than one with a mode, because an operator that does
    ; not say which modification it performs reads the same as the other two.
    (operator (name Insert)             (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    (operator (name Update)             (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming))
    (operator (name Delete)             (arity 1) (kind pipeline) (edges streaming)))

  ; Implementation rules: the physical operator each logical operator lowers to.
  ; Increment 0 is single-candidate (one physical op per logical op); a rule set
  ; with alternatives is what the Cascades search will explore in a later unit.
  ; The lowering consults these, so the logical->physical choice is data, not code.
  (implementation-rules
    (rule (logical Scan)    (physical SeqScan))
    (rule (logical Filter)  (physical Filter))
    (rule (logical Project) (physical Project))
    ; Two candidates for a join: the planner costs both - including any enforcers
    ; each would cause - and keeps the cheaper.
    (rule (logical Join)    (physical HashJoin))
    (rule (logical Join)    (physical MergeJoin))
    ; The keyless fallback. Applicability (not cost) is what selects it: the two
    ; keyed joins are not applicable without an equi-key.
    (rule (logical Join)    (physical NestedLoopJoin))
    ; Single-candidate for now. A TopN that fuses a Limit over a Sort is the
    ; obvious second candidate and is deliberately absent: it fuses TWO logical
    ; nodes into one physical operator, which needs a transformation rule, and the
    ; memo is still one group per logical node. Adding TopN as an implementation
    ; rule for either node alone would be wrong.
    (rule (logical Sort)    (physical Sort))
    (rule (logical Limit)   (physical Limit))
    (rule (logical Aggregate) (physical HashAggregate))
    (rule (logical Aggregate) (physical StreamingAggregate))
    ; Applicability, not cost, picks between these and the two above: a plain
    ; aggregate computes ONE key combination and this computes several.
    (rule (logical Aggregate) (physical HashGroupingSets))
    (rule (logical Window)    (physical Window))
    (rule (logical Distinct)  (physical HashDistinct))
    (rule (logical Distinct)  (physical StreamingDistinct))
    (rule (logical SetOp)     (physical UnionAll))
    (rule (logical SetOp)     (physical HashSetOp))
    (rule (logical Values)    (physical ValuesScan))
    (rule (logical SemiJoin)  (physical HashSemiJoin))
    (rule (logical SemiJoin)  (physical NestedLoopSemiJoin))
    (rule (logical AntiJoin)  (physical HashAntiJoin))
    (rule (logical AntiJoin)  (physical NestedLoopAntiJoin))
    ; Single-candidate, and not for want of looking: a fixpoint is evaluated one
    ; way, and the semi-naive refinement is a detail inside that operator rather
    ; than a rival to cost against it.
    (rule (logical RecursiveCTE)      (physical RecursiveFixpoint))
    (rule (logical WorkingTableScan)  (physical WorkingTableScan))
    (rule (logical CreateTableAs)     (physical CreateTableAs))
    (rule (logical Insert)            (physical Insert))
    (rule (logical Update)            (physical Update))
    (rule (logical Delete)            (physical Delete))
    ; RETURNING is a projection over the rows the modification affected, which is
    ; what Project already is. It gets no operator of its own: a Project above an
    ; Insert can only be a RETURNING clause, there being no other reason for one
    ; to sit there, and a second way to say projection would need a mapping.
    (rule (logical Returning)         (physical Project)))

  ; Where exhaustive search stops being affordable (design D5). Past this many
  ; joins the planner falls back to a bounded enumeration - a worse plan chosen by
  ; the SAME cost model, rather than a better plan that arrives too late.
  ;
  ; The number here is a placeholder, not a derived value. Deriving it from the
  ; planning-time budget is open question 5; what is settled is that it lives in
  ; the spec rather than in a source file, so it can be tuned per profile without
  ; a rebuild.
  (search-budget (max-join-count 8))

  ; The reference ExecutionCapabilityProfile: what a competent hardware-native
  ; engine is assumed able to execute. It ships now so the planner is usable and
  ; testable before any execution engine exists; a real engine later publishes
  ; its own profile in this same shape.
  (capability-profile
    (name reference)
    (executes SeqScan Filter Project HashJoin MergeJoin NestedLoopJoin
              Sort FormatConvert Limit HashAggregate StreamingAggregate Window
              HashDistinct StreamingDistinct UnionAll HashSetOp ValuesScan
              HashSemiJoin HashAntiJoin NestedLoopSemiJoin NestedLoopAntiJoin
              HashGroupingSets RecursiveFixpoint WorkingTableScan CreateTableAs
              Insert Update Delete Exchange)))
